  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions are probable Monday night \ninto early Tuesday morning over the western & central Alleghenies\n\nAs of mid evening, partial clearing into our western areas, but\nthe cold front is lagging to the northwest over southern\nOntario. A rather deep upper level trough over northern New York\nstate will deepen overnight, with a tight 500 mb height gradient\nby morning. This will lead to bands of lower SC clouds and gusty\nwinds early Monday, but 500 mb heights come up fast later in the\nday. The rising 500 mb heights will aid in a cold night Monday \nNight, as winds weaken, and clouds fall apart with warming aloft\nand much drier air being advected into central PA.\n\nMore detail below.\n\nThe coldest air of the early Autumn season will push southeast \ninto Central PA early this week with frost/freeze conditions \nexpected Monday night into early Tuesday morning over much of\nthe western and central Alleghenies (northwest of I81). We\nissued a freeze watch for 4 counties in the NW Alleghenies\nleaning a bit colder vs. NBM/MOS blend. Placement of sfc high\nbecomes somewhat less favorable for frost/freeze Tuesday night. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Extended stretch of dry and seasonable Fall \nweather this week\n\nLow clouds entrenched on the eastside of the Allegheny Front \nwith embedded areas of light rain, drizzle and fog will \ngradually improve tonight into Monday as the mean low level flow\nshifts from the east/southeast to the west/northwest behind a\nsecondary cold front. The colder WNW flow could bring a couple \nof rain showers downwind of Lake Erie tonight into early Monday.\n\nOtherwise, we continue to have high confidence in a prolonged \nstretch of dry and seasonable Fall wx this week (Mon-Fri) as a \nlarge area of Canadian high pressure dominates the pattern.\n\nThe next chance of showers looks to be next Sunday. Sunday has\noften been the more active weather day this summer and early\ninto the fall now.",
